as a child growing up in Indiana in the  Midwest I saw this movie  then I could count I was an  african-american child obviously in a  family of ten kids my mother my father  my grandmother 13 of us living in a  3-bedroom trailer and we were the only  African American family for miles around  and so I understood the black and white  notion the black and white tension that  existed in this film and indeed in my  own community some years ago and so I  saw this movie countless times as a  child I always felt then not knowing  much about the Hollywood process that  you could not make a movie that was more  perfectly cast I felt that as a child  growing up in Indiana but when I use  later moved to Los Angeles after college  to work for the late great mayor and the  first and all the african-american mayor  to date of this city Tom Bradley I yes I  had the occasion to meet Gregory Peck  and Brock Peters countless times they  were both dear friends of the mayor and  had many times to sit and to talk with  him and to pick their brains and to to  get to know them just a wee bit and once  I got to know them in my own way as  Friends of the mayor I really came to  understand how perfectly cast this movie  really was because the one thing that  Gregory Peck and Brock Peters did not  have to act out was their humanity and I  was so delighted as you often know I'm I  won't call names but I do this every day  so sometimes you meet people and they  are not as advertised shall we say in  this town and so I was quite delighted  to know that the man who I'd come to  know on-screen in this brilliant classic  film were as advertised their humanity  was something they did not have to act  out and as I look back on it now it gave  me a brand-new working definition of  what it means in this town to be a true  authentic character actor I think this  film reminds us of a number of things I  was caught in a number of conversations  in the lobby prior to making it in here  and more than one person said to me in  his or her own way  with regard to the relevance of this  film 50 years later what are the chances  no matter what you think of the case no  matter the outcome it as we get it  started a national conversation this  killing of Trayvon Martin in Florida and  what are the chances that 50 years later  as we sit here in Beverly Hills to  screen this classic film about the black  and white tension that has existed for  so long in this country what are the  chances that this day in Florida George  Zimmerman would have been arrested what  are the chances that we sit here tonight  to watch this film all these years later  about that tension sadly that still  exists in our country what I celebrate  about Hollywood at its best let me  underscore that what I celebrate  celebrate that is about Hollywood at its  best is that the killer mockingbird  reminds us that films don't just have to  entertain they can do more than  entertain they can entertain and they  can empower and they can inspire and  they can elevate the discourse in this  country when you see a film like the  Kill a Mockingbird you get reminded that  Hollywood oftentimes is so focused on  monetizing that we forget about  humanizing and this film underscores  that we can both monetize and humanize  at the same time this movie as you well  know set against the backdrop of the  segregated south it celebrates the power  of good in the face of evil or as I like  to say simply put it celebrates that  love wins love wins thanks to one of the  most cherished books in all of American  literature the film adaptation of Harper  Lee's magnum opus also gives us one of  the great movie heroes of our time is  the president under the other day  Atticus Finch played with sublime  strength by Gregory Peck Atticus Finch  became the living embodiment of the core  values that will under attack in this  country  in the age of Jim Crow and might I add  Jane crow what it must have meant back  in 1962 to Negroes  as we 